    /**
     * Groups whose draws are already recorded but whose command buffer may still be in flight.
     * <p>
     * Destroying the mesh buffers immediately after recording them is a use-after-free as far as the GPU is
     * concerned: the handoff submits later in the same pass, so the buffers must outlive it. Keeping one frame's
     * worth and releasing it at the next flush is enough, because a pass only starts after the previous one signalled.
     */
    private final java.util.List<ImmediateGroup> retiredImmediateGroups = new java.util.ArrayList<ImmediateGroup>();
    /** Scratch for the flush pass's kept groups; compacted back into {@link #immediateGroups} once per pass. */
    private final java.util.List<ImmediateGroup> keptImmediateGroups = new java.util.ArrayList<ImmediateGroup>();
    private VulkanImmediatePipeline immediatePipeline;
    /** The matrices in force when the pending batches were built; the flush runs a frame later. */
    /**
     * The scratch the per-call matrix builders fill in place of a fresh {@code new float[16]}: {@code ortho},
     * {@code frustum} and the perspective helper each built a throwaway array per call on a path the matrix census
     * counts in hundreds per frame. The consumers ({@code immediateState.loadMatrix}/{@code multiplyMatrix}) copy
     * the values out before returning, so sharing one array is safe.
     * <p>
     * <b>Every builder must fill all sixteen elements</b>, and that rule was broken here in a way that cost the main
     * menu its background: {@code ortho} writes {0,5,10,12,13,14,15} and the perspective builders write
     * {0,5,10,11,14}, so a perspective built after an ortho kept the ortho's translation in [12] and [13] and its 1
     * in [15]. A GL projection's [12]/[13] are the x/y terms of <em>w</em>, not a translation, so the panorama's
     * cube was divided by {@code w = -x + y - 0.101z + 1} instead of {@code w = -z}: measured on the main menu
     * (round 226, jar f9d64a91e), the six faces' clip-space corners came out at NDC x[-4.28..-0.08] y[0.25..3.12]
     * and painted 0.8% of the 256x256 box per frame, so the copy never repainted its rectangle and the menu's
     * background stayed the blur's own fixed point. The world was unaffected because its projection follows another
     * perspective rather than the GUI's ortho - which is exactly why the symptom looked like "the menu only".
     */
    private final float[] matrixScratch = new float[16];

    /**
     * Which builders clear the shared scratch before writing it, from {@code -Dceltium.vulkan.scratchZero} as a comma
     * list of {@code ortho}, {@code perspective}, {@code frustum} and {@code glu}.
     * <p>
     * <b>Both directions of the inheritance are measured now; only one of them is in the default.</b> A perspective
     * built after an ortho kept the ortho's {@code [12]}, {@code [13]} and {@code [15]} - the main menu's panorama was
     * divided by {@code w = -x + y - 0.101z + 1} (round 226, jar f9d64a91e) - and, in the other direction, an
     * <b>ortho</b> built after a perspective keeps its {@code [11] = -1}: a GL projection's {@code [11]} is the z term
     * of <em>w</em>, which is -1 for a perspective and 0 for an orthographic projection, so the GUI's ortho came out
     * with {@code w = 1 - z}. Every GUI vertex is a pre-transformed quad in GUI space with {@code z} around -2000
     * (OPT-18b bakes the model-view into the vertices and records an identity model-view), so the divide collapsed each
     * quad to the screen centre: measured on leg r212-world-hud20/21 (jar 5d83e1df6482d825), every one of frame 130's
     * 23 HUD groups reports {@code ndc x[-0.00..0.00] y[-0.00..0.00]} with a correct ortho
     * ({@code m00=0.005 m11=-0.008}), the vignette's vertices {@code [0,240,-2090; 427,240,-2090; 427,0,-2090;
     * 0,0,-2090]} all clip to {@code (0,0,0.5,2091)} - {@code cw = 2091 = 1 - z} - and leg r212-world-hud19's
     * per-window readbacks of frame 130 show the frame image's hotbar band and crosshair patch unchanged across all
     * seven windows, so the draws rasterised nothing. The captured matrix confirms both halves at once: the remap in
     * {@code closeCurrentImmediateGroup} turns the inherited {@code [11] = -1} into {@code m22 = -0.501} and
     * {@code m32 = -0.5}, which is what the bounds line printed.
     * <p>
     * The property remains the bisect instrument: {@code frustum} and {@code glu} are deliberately not in the default,
     * because zeroing all four turned every presented frame black in round 226 and neither has been measured to need
     * it. <b>{@code ortho} is in the default as of round 248</b>, together with {@link #SELF_COMPOSITE_SKIP} and for a
     * stated reason: with the ortho repaired the HUD's quads project correctly - leg r212-world-hud23/26
     * (jar 578fbee77c33d8f4 / 3cc28cb1a40e0c32) reports {@code proj=[m00=0.005 m11=-0.008 m22=0.0 m32=-0.5]} and frame
     * 140's window 5 moves the frame image's hotbar band from the world's colour to the hotbar's own
     * {@code (66,54,41)} - and the game's finished-frame composite then paints that same image black unless it is
     * dropped, which the skip does by name. The two together are the measured pair: the leg with both reports
     * {@code presentedMean=71.3}, {@code hotbarBand=(72,60,49)} at the present, and a presented PNG carrying the
     * hotbar, the crosshair and the HUD text over the world (distinct 11932, was 2440 before the HUD and 1 with the
     * ortho repair alone).
     */
    private static boolean scratchZeroWanted(String builder) {
        String list = System.getProperty("celtium.vulkan.scratchZero", "perspective,ortho");
        for (String part : list.split(",")) {
            if (part.trim().equalsIgnoreCase(builder)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
    private final float[] glProjection = new float[16];
    private final float[] glModelView = new float[16];

    /**
     * A draw-state change is a boundary for the coalesced font run, exactly like a matrix change is: the run's quads
     * were appended under the old state and the group's capture reads the live one at submit, so a run that outlives
     * the change is drawn under state it was never written for. The measured case is the vanilla tooltip: Forge
     * brackets it with disableDepth()/enableDepth(), the run holding the shadow pass is still pending when the second
     * call arrives, and a flush that happens after it submits the run depth-tested - 585 of 797 shadow pixels then
     * lost their main stroke (73.4 %, instrument font-shadow-main-check.py). Flushing <em>before</em> the mutation -
     * not at the next append, which is already too late - keeps the run with the state it was captured under.
     *
     * <p>The same rule is applied by the stub for the callers that bypass this mirror (raw {@code GL11} calls land in
     * {@code CeltiumGlStub}'s records, which the group capture reads live for blend/cull/colour-mask/scissor), and it
     * is why the flush is a no-op when the value does not change: {@code GlStateManager} calls its setters on its own
     * cache misses too, and a no-op must not split a run.
     */
    /** A/B-only switch for proving the exact same JAR fixes deferred font-state ordering. Default on. */
    private static final boolean FONT_DRAW_STATE_BOUNDARY_ENABLED =
            !"false".equalsIgnoreCase(System.getProperty("celtium.vulkan.fontDrawStateBoundary", "true"));

    private void celtium$fontDrawStateBoundary() {
        if (!FONT_DRAW_STATE_BOUNDARY_ENABLED) {
            return;
        }
        // The GlStateManager setters call this on their own cache misses, ~4,000 times a frame, and 9 of those calls
        // have a run to submit. With neither a pending run nor glyphs held by the capture, the flush would run its
        // pre-flush hook (which finds nothing), find nothing pending and return - so the answer is known without
        // asking. Measured before this: fontFlushCalls 3,961.8/frame against fontRunsStarted 9.0.
        if (!this.fontBatch.celtium$hasWork()) {
            return;
        }
        celtium$flushPendingFontBatch("state-change");
    }

    @Override
    public void setBlendEnabled(boolean enabled) {
        if (this.immediateState.blendEnabled() != enabled) {
            celtium$fontDrawStateBoundary();
        }
        this.immediateState.blendEnabled(enabled);
        this.immediateStatistics.noteImmediateStateCalls();
    }

    @Override
    public void setBlendFunc(int sourceRgb, int destRgb, int sourceAlpha, int destAlpha) {
        celtium$fontDrawStateBoundary();
        this.immediateState.blendFunc(sourceRgb, destRgb, sourceAlpha, destAlpha);
        this.immediateStatistics.noteImmediateStateCalls();
    }

